  "s1ref": {
   "id": "s1ref",
   "page": 10,
   "kind": "instruction",
   "tab": "Step 1",
   "overline": "Step 1 · before Exercises 6 to 8",
   "title": "Other forms, positions, and size",
   "lead": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": [
      "The check before Exercise 6"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "Read the problem definition in Exercise 2 against two questions. Could someone who disagrees about the cause still accept the problem definition as a description? Could that person say what would have to change for the problem definition to stop being true? If either answer is no, the problem definition is carrying a cause, a blame, or a solution, and needs to be rewritten in Exercise 6. If both answers are yes, copy the first attempt across to Exercise 6 as it stands. Either way, the agreed problem definition is always written in Exercise 6, because it is the one every later step reads."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "When too much and too little does not fit"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "The form on page ",
      {
       "ref": "s1instr"
      },
      " is the default, not the only one. Take these in order, and choose one only with a line saying why the default did not fit. All of them carry the same five parts: the thing, the direction or the gap, who it affects, since when, and why it matters."
     ]
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       [
        "Form"
       ],
       [
        "Use it when, and how to write it"
       ]
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Mismatch"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "The quantity is right, but it is in the wrong place, at the wrong time, or with the wrong people. ",
         {
          "i": [
           "X exists, but not where (or when, or for whom) it is needed: it is at A while the need is at B."
          ]
         }
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Falling short of a standard"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "The thing exists and is counted, but fails a rule, a promise, or a quality that was set. ",
         {
          "i": [
           "X falls short of Y: the standard says A, the practice is B."
          ]
         }
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Not happened yet"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "A risk, or a capacity that does not exist at all. There is no series to be too high or too low. ",
         {
          "i": [
           "If A continues, B becomes likely, and nothing currently prevents it."
          ]
         }
        ]
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "The five positions, and what each one changes"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "Your position decides what the analysis ends in, so it is chosen once and carried through."
     ]
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       [
        "Position"
       ],
       [
        "What the analysis ends in"
       ]
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        [
         "Analysing from outside"
        ],
        [
         "Findings. Your own influence is not part of the analysis"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         "Acting for an organisation or a cause"
        ],
        [
         "A target and an ask. Your own influence is part of the analysis"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         "Advising someone who decides"
        ],
        [
         "Options with a recommendation. The reach that counts is the client's, not yours"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         "Deciding, or your organisation does"
        ],
        [
         "A decision. What is inside your own control is looked at first"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         "Affected by this"
        ],
        [
         "A route through an organisation or a coalition, because the power available is mostly power with others"
        ]
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "If the problem is too large, or too small"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "Exercise 3 is how you know. If you cannot say where the problem starts and stops, or cannot foresee about a dozen things that drive it, it is too large. If almost nothing drives it, it is too small."
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      {
       "b": [
        "Narrow"
       ]
      },
      " by place, by population, by a single decision, or by period. ",
      {
       "b": [
        "Widen"
       ]
      },
      " by asking why the situation persists, or by taking in the decision that sets the conditions for it. The size question in Exercise 3 is a judgement rather than a rule: it asks whether the problem is small enough to work on and still large enough to be worth explaining."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "How others describe it, in Exercise 7"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "Find how the problem is currently described by those with power over it: a policy document, a press release, a minister's answer, an annual report, a minutes entry. Write their description in their words, not yours, and then write what that description takes for granted and what it leaves out. Give the source. One description is enough here; the others come later."
     ]
    },
    {
     "opposite": [
      "Opposite: Exercises 6 to 8"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
