  "s2instr1": {
   "id": "s2instr1",
   "page": 12,
   "kind": "instruction",
   "tab": "Step 2",
   "overline": "Step 2 · before you begin",
   "title": "Set the boundary and the perspectives",
   "lead": [
    "To decide what is inside the system and what is left out, and whose views count. This is where the case stops being a topic and becomes a system with edges."
   ],
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": [
      "Four words this step uses precisely"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      {
       "b": [
        "Actor."
       ]
      },
      " Someone who can act in this system or is acted upon by it. Any one of four tests admits an actor: ",
      {
       "b": [
        "1"
       ]
      },
      " they do something that affects the problem, ",
      {
       "b": [
        "2"
       ]
      },
      " they are affected by what the system produces, ",
      {
       "b": [
        "3"
       ]
      },
      " they hold something others in the system need, or ",
      {
       "b": [
        "4"
       ]
      },
      " they can start or stop a change. Two things are not actors: a category with no agency, such as the weather, the market, or society, which is a condition rather than an actor; and a named individual, unless the thing held belongs to that person rather than to their organisation."
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      {
       "b": [
        "Layer."
       ]
      },
      " A set of actors who work within the same scope of decision-making or implementation, meaning that what any of them decides applies to the same territory, population, or part of the organisation. Layers are ordered by how far a decision reaches, from the widest scope of decision to the narrowest, and by nothing else. They are not ranks, not importance, and not a chain of command. They belong to the system, not to you."
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      {
       "b": [
        "Actor type."
       ]
      },
      " What kind of actor this is, in this case. The list is written by the participant, at most five types, and as a guide no more than one type per three actors. An actor that seems to need two types usually means the type list is wrong rather than the actor."
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      {
       "b": [
        "Stakeholder"
       ]
      },
      " is not used in this process. Everything inside the system is an actor."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "Drawing the boundary"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "Almost everything is connected to almost everything else, so \"is this related?\" admits everything. The test is difference, not relevance: ",
      {
       "b": [
        "would including this change what the analysis concludes?"
       ]
      },
      " A candidate goes inside if any one of three things is true. The candidate can increase or decrease the quantity named in the agreed problem definition. The candidate is on the receiving end of the excess or deficit of that quantity, meaning the candidate is part of who the problem is for. Or the candidate sets the rules the first two work under."
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "Everything left out is marked ",
      {
       "b": [
        "F"
       ]
      },
      " fixed, ",
      {
       "b": [
        "O"
       ]
      },
      " out of reach, or ",
      {
       "b": [
        "I"
       ]
      },
      " irrelevant, with one line of reason. Fixed means it will not change during the period being analysed. Out of reach means it could change but nobody in this case can affect it. Irrelevant means including it would not change what the analysis concludes."
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "The boundary stays provisional. Everything marked F or O is written down rather than forgotten, because a thing assumed fixed that turns out to move is the commonest reason an intervention fails."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "How many actors"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "About a dozen. Past roughly fifteen, either the granularity or the boundary has gone wrong: individual officials are being listed where the department that decides is the actor, or the boundary has taken in layers where nobody passes any of the three tests above. Check granularity first."
     ]
    },
    {
     "opposite": [
      "Opposite: Exercises 1 to 4"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
