  "s3instr": {
   "id": "s3instr",
   "page": 20,
   "kind": "instruction",
   "tab": "Step 3",
   "overline": "Step 3 · before you draw",
   "title": "Describe behaviour over time",
   "lead": [
    "To show the pattern the system produces. This is the first step that looks at the case as something that has been happening rather than as something that is the case."
   ],
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": [
      "Behaviour means the system's, not anyone's conduct"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "It is the path a quantity has taken over time, and nothing about how people acted. Writing about officials being slow is answering a different question."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "What to graph"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      {
       "b": [
        "What is measured is fixed. How you measure it is yours."
       ]
      },
      " The thing the graph is about is the one the agreed problem definition names: the thing there is too much or too little of. That does not change here, because changing it is how a case quietly changes subject, and nobody notices afterwards because everything that follows is consistent with whatever was graphed."
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "The measure is a separate choice, and it is yours. One thing can usually be counted several ways, and you take whichever count has a history you can draw: a waiting list, the number of people in emergency accommodation, and nights of shelter used are three measures of one thing. Write down which measure you are using, and why that one. If no measure of the named thing can be found at all, the thing named is not a quantity, which is a return to Step 1 rather than a fault in this step."
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "If the agreed problem definition used one of the other forms, graph the quantity that the definition compares against something. For a ",
      {
       "b": [
        "mismatch"
       ]
      },
      ", graph the amount available in the place where the need is. For ",
      {
       "b": [
        "falling short of a standard"
       ]
      },
      ", graph the measured level of the thing the standard is about. For ",
      {
       "b": [
        "not happened yet"
       ]
      },
      ", there is no series to draw, so graph the nearest quantity that does have a history: the pressure that would produce the risk, or the capacity that would absorb it, and say why that is the nearest one available."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "The period goes back to the last time the system changed"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "Back to the last major change (a reform, a crisis, a new instrument, a change of government with a policy attached), or roughly ten years, whichever is longer. Not for thoroughness: a period that starts after the last reform shows the system settling down, which looks like stability, and an explanation built for stability explains the wrong thing. Use intervals as coarse as the evidence allows. Yearly is usually enough, and monthly usually produces noise you will then try to explain."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "This step tests Step 1"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "Step 1's second rule was to name a thing that can rise or fall. Here that claim is put to the test."
     ]
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       [
        "If this happens"
       ],
       [
        "Go back to"
       ]
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        [
         "The line cannot be drawn, because the quantity does not move"
        ],
        [
         "Step 1, page ",
         {
          "ref": "s1work2"
         },
         ", and rename the thing. What was named is a condition or a judgement, not a quantity"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         "The line can be drawn, but it is not the problem"
        ],
        [
         "Step 1, page ",
         {
          "ref": "s1work2"
         },
         ". The problem definition named a proxy"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         "The period has to reach outside your boundary"
        ],
        [
         "Step 2, page ",
         {
          "ref": "s2work1"
         },
         ", and reopen the exclusions"
        ]
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "This is the commonest return in the whole process, and going back here costs very little."
     ]
    },
    {
     "opposite": [
      "Opposite: Exercises 1 to 4"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
