  "s3ref": {
   "id": "s3ref",
   "page": 22,
   "kind": "instruction",
   "tab": "Step 3",
   "overline": "Step 3 · before Exercises 5 to 7",
   "title": "Shapes, evidence, and the system problem definition",
   "lead": null,
   "blocks": [
    {
     "h": [
      "The six shapes"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "Pick the one that describes your graph, and then describe why your graph is that shape. A shape you choose is a claim about the system. A shape you describe in your own words is a caption."
     ]
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       [
        "Shape"
       ],
       [
        "What the line does"
       ]
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Steady rise or fall"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "Moves one way at roughly the same rate"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Accelerating"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "Moves one way, faster and faster"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Levelling off"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "Moves one way, then flattens towards a limit"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Up and down"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "Repeatedly overshoots and comes back"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Rise then collapse"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "Grows, turns, and falls below where it started"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Flat"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "Does not move, despite the pressure on it"
        ]
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "template": [
      {
       "b": [
        "Flat is a pattern, not a failure."
       ]
      },
      " A quantity that stays put while people push on it is being held there by something, and naming what holds it is the work that follows. Most problems that have survived several attempts are this shape."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "Evidence is labelled, not ranked"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "A line drawn from documented accounts is a legitimate graph. What matters is that the reader can tell which kind each part of the line is. Where the line changes kind, mark the change."
     ]
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       [
        "Kind"
       ],
       [
        "What you write beside the line"
       ]
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Measured"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "The source and the years it covers"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Documented"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "The documents, and what each one establishes about direction or timing"
        ]
       ],
       [
        [
         {
          "b": [
           "Estimated"
          ]
         }
        ],
        [
         "What the estimate rests on, and the words \"this line is an estimate\""
        ]
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "The system problem definition"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "One sentence naming the pattern. It is a different sentence from the problem definition on page ",
      {
       "ref": "s1work2"
      },
      ": that one says what is wrong, and this one says what the system keeps doing."
     ]
    },
    {
     "template": [
      "Since ",
      {
       "b": [
        "[when]"
       ]
      },
      ", ",
      {
       "b": [
        "[the quantity]"
       ]
      },
      " has ",
      {
       "b": [
        "[shape]"
       ]
      },
      ", and ",
      {
       "b": [
        "[what has been tried]"
       ]
      },
      " has not changed it."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": [
      "The two futures"
     ]
    },
    {
     "p": [
      "Optional, and drawn on the same axes as the line. One dotted line for what you expect to happen if nothing changes, and one for what the desired change from Step 1 would look like if it happened. Neither is a prediction. Both are drawn before anything has been chosen, which is the point: they fix what better would look like as a shape, while nobody is yet attached to a particular way of getting there."
     ]
    },
    {
     "opposite": [
      "Opposite: Exercises 5 to 7"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
